1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,351
Kitchen salt.

2
00:00:01,351 --> 00:00:02,385
Classroom chalk.

3
00:00:02,385 --> 00:00:04,153
Lime for whitewashing a wall.

4
00:00:04,553 --> 00:00:06,250
All three are ionic solids.

5
00:00:06,250 --> 00:00:08,134
Hard, and very hard to melt.

6
00:00:08,584 --> 00:00:10,658
Diya heats some salt in a dry pan.

7
00:00:10,658 --> 00:00:12,322
Sugar would melt in minutes.

8
00:00:12,322 --> 00:00:13,858
The salt just sits there.

9
00:00:14,208 --> 00:00:17,601
Salt melts only at eight
hundred and one degrees Celsius.

10
00:00:17,601 --> 00:00:20,040
Something holds it together very tightly.

11
00:00:20,540 --> 00:00:24,989
By the end, you will see where that
strength comes from: the lattice enthalpy.

12
00:00:25,639 --> 00:00:27,163
First, recall the idea.

13
00:00:27,163 --> 00:00:31,131
In an ionic bond, one atom gives
electrons and the other takes them.

14
00:00:31,481 --> 00:00:33,960
Think of a tug of war between two kids.

15
00:00:33,960 --> 00:00:35,824
One is weak, one is strong.

16
00:00:35,824 --> 00:00:37,733
The strong one takes the rope.

17
00:00:38,133 --> 00:00:40,786
Sodium has a low ionization enthalpy.

18
00:00:40,786 --> 00:00:43,342
It lets go of its electron easily.

19
00:00:43,742 --> 00:00:47,193
Chlorine has a large negative
electron gain enthalpy.

20
00:00:47,193 --> 00:00:49,969
It grabs an electron and releases energy.

21
00:00:50,369 --> 00:00:54,866
So ionic bonds form most easily between
such pairs: metals from the left,

22
00:00:54,866 --> 00:00:56,644
non metals from the right.

23
00:00:57,294 --> 00:00:59,727
Now the energy, on our molecule bench.

24
00:00:59,727 --> 00:01:03,992
Making a sodium ion costs four hundred
and ninety five point eight kilojoules

25
00:01:03,992 --> 00:01:04,892
per mole.

26
00:01:05,089 --> 00:01:09,212
Chlorine taking an electron gives back
three hundred and forty eight point

27
00:01:09,212 --> 00:01:10,112
seven.

28
00:01:10,174 --> 00:01:11,074
Add them.

29
00:01:10,878 --> 00:01:14,285
We are still short by one hundred
and forty seven point one.

30
00:01:14,285 --> 00:01:16,937
Making the ions costs
more than it gives back!

31
00:01:17,337 --> 00:01:19,133
So why does salt form at all?

32
00:01:19,133 --> 00:01:20,828
Watch the ions come together.

33
00:01:21,228 --> 00:01:25,362
Each sodium ion is surrounded by chloride
ions, and each chloride by sodium

34
00:01:25,362 --> 00:01:27,896
ions, in a neat three dimensional pattern.

35
00:01:27,896 --> 00:01:29,530
This is a crystal lattice.

36
00:01:29,930 --> 00:01:34,267
Every time opposite charges click into
place, like magnets, energy is released.

37
00:01:34,267 --> 00:01:37,045
Seven hundred and eighty
eight kilojoules per mole.

38
00:01:37,445 --> 00:01:40,856
That big release pays the
cost, with plenty left over.

39
00:01:40,856 --> 00:01:42,771
The lattice makes salt stable.

40
00:01:43,421 --> 00:01:44,338
Side by side.

41
00:01:44,338 --> 00:01:47,353
Making the gas ions costs
one hundred and forty seven.

42
00:01:47,353 --> 00:01:50,613
Building the lattice releases
seven hundred and eighty eight.

43
00:01:51,013 --> 00:01:52,123
Pause and think.

44
00:01:52,123 --> 00:01:54,721
Which is bigger, the cost or the release?

45
00:01:57,921 --> 00:01:58,821
The release!

46
00:01:58,764 --> 00:02:02,752
That is why the ionic solid is stable,
even though making the ions costs

47
00:02:02,752 --> 00:02:03,652
energy.

48
00:02:03,975 --> 00:02:06,150
Now the definition, step by step.

49
00:02:06,500 --> 00:02:07,400
Step one.

50
00:02:07,385 --> 00:02:12,103
Lattice enthalpy is the energy needed
to completely separate one mole of

51
00:02:12,103 --> 00:02:15,320
a solid ionic compound
into its gaseous ions.

52
00:02:16,070 --> 00:02:16,970
Step two.

53
00:02:16,799 --> 00:02:20,774
For sodium chloride, it is seven
hundred and eighty eight kilojoules per

54
00:02:20,774 --> 00:02:21,674
mole.

55
00:02:21,272 --> 00:02:24,011
That much energy pulls
the crystal fully apart.

56
00:02:24,761 --> 00:02:25,661
Step three.

57
00:02:25,557 --> 00:02:27,149
Bigger charges pull harder.

58
00:02:27,149 --> 00:02:30,896
Ions with charges of two pull far
more strongly than ions with charges

59
00:02:30,896 --> 00:02:31,796
of one.

60
00:02:32,269 --> 00:02:33,169
Step four.

61
00:02:33,135 --> 00:02:37,066
Smaller ions sit closer, and
closer charges pull harder too.

62
00:02:37,066 --> 00:02:40,599
Small size and big charge
mean a big lattice enthalpy.

63
00:02:41,349 --> 00:02:42,274
Remember this.

64
00:02:42,274 --> 00:02:44,455
The lattice pays for making the ions.

65
00:02:44,455 --> 00:02:46,531
Strong lattice, stable ionic solid.

66
00:02:47,231 --> 00:02:49,221
Now, one worked example.

67
00:02:49,221 --> 00:02:52,343
Compare sodium chloride
and magnesium oxide.

68
00:02:52,643 --> 00:02:56,614
Sodium chloride has ions with
charges of plus one and minus one.

69
00:02:57,114 --> 00:03:00,823
Magnesium oxide has M g
two plus and O two minus.

70
00:03:00,823 --> 00:03:03,097
Twice the charge on each ion.

71
00:03:03,847 --> 00:03:08,244
Magnesium and oxide ions are also
smaller than sodium and chloride ions.

72
00:03:08,994 --> 00:03:11,488
Both changes make the pull stronger.

73
00:03:11,488 --> 00:03:15,098
So magnesium oxide has a
much higher lattice enthalpy.

74
00:03:15,848 --> 00:03:20,077
That is why magnesium oxide melts at
about two thousand eight hundred degrees,

75
00:03:20,077 --> 00:03:22,555
while salt melts at eight hundred and one.

76
00:03:23,205 --> 00:03:26,581
Boards like ISC also ask
for the Born Haber cycle.

77
00:03:26,581 --> 00:03:31,053
It adds up every step: turning solid
sodium into a gas, splitting chlorine

78
00:03:31,053 --> 00:03:34,439
molecules, making the ions,
and building the lattice.

79
00:03:34,839 --> 00:03:36,779
They also ask for the properties.

80
00:03:36,779 --> 00:03:40,616
Ionic solids are hard, melt at high
temperatures, and many dissolve in

81
00:03:40,616 --> 00:03:41,516
water.

82
00:03:41,540 --> 00:03:45,308
They conduct electricity when melted
or dissolved, because then the ions

83
00:03:45,308 --> 00:03:46,453
are free to move.

84
00:03:47,103 --> 00:03:48,567
Now, the exam trap.

85
00:03:48,917 --> 00:03:52,506
Students think ionic bonds form because
making the ions releases energy.

86
00:03:52,906 --> 00:03:56,818
But making sodium and chloride ions
costs one hundred and forty seven point

87
00:03:56,818 --> 00:03:57,718
one.

88
00:03:57,254 --> 00:03:59,680
It is the lattice that makes it worth it.

89
00:04:00,330 --> 00:04:01,666
Where is this asked?

90
00:04:01,666 --> 00:04:05,684
Expect questions on the energy steps
of an ionic bond, and on comparing

91
00:04:05,684 --> 00:04:07,935
lattice enthalpies by charge and size.

92
00:04:08,535 --> 00:04:09,733
Exam level now.

93
00:04:09,733 --> 00:04:12,849
Which compound has the
highest lattice enthalpy?

94
00:04:12,849 --> 00:04:14,097
Pause and solve.

95
00:04:19,497 --> 00:04:22,098
The answer is C, magnesium oxide.

96
00:04:22,598 --> 00:04:25,331
Its ions carry charges of two, not one.

97
00:04:25,831 --> 00:04:29,084
Magnesium and oxide ions are
also the smallest ions here.

98
00:04:29,584 --> 00:04:33,857
In the others the charges are only
one, so the pull is much weaker.

99
00:04:34,457 --> 00:04:35,997
Here is your recap.

100
00:04:35,997 --> 00:04:37,580
Screenshot this card.

101
00:04:40,780 --> 00:04:42,966
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

102
00:04:43,366 --> 00:04:44,266
One.

103
00:04:43,827 --> 00:04:45,399
What is lattice enthalpy?

104
00:04:46,199 --> 00:04:47,099
Two.

105
00:04:46,749 --> 00:04:51,510
Why do ionization and electron gain
alone not explain why salt forms?

106
00:04:52,310 --> 00:04:53,210
Three.

107
00:04:52,982 --> 00:04:58,056
Which has the higher lattice enthalpy,
sodium chloride or magnesium oxide,

108
00:04:58,056 --> 00:04:58,956
and why?

109
00:04:59,803 --> 00:05:04,052
Next time: what happens when neither
atom will give up an electron?

110
00:05:04,052 --> 00:05:07,215
We find out in covalent
bond and Lewis structures.
